      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Glaciers moving across bedrock abrade the surface and create linear scratches (striations) on rocks.",
      "evidence": "Glacial geology textbooks describe basal debris acting as sandpaper that produces striations during glacier advance.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The presence of such striations on rocks is a primary indicator that a glacier once covered the area.",
      "evidence": "Field geologists routinely use bedrock striations as the most direct evidence of past glaciation in mapping studies.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Fossils, moss cover, and deep burial are not uniquely tied to glaciation and therefore are unreliable as primary glacial indicators.",
      "evidence": "Fossils occur in many sedimentary environments; moss grows in diverse climates; rocks become buried by sedimentation independent of ice movement.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Even though some fossils (e.g., dropstones) can be associated with glacial transport, they are sporadic and require corroborating evidence, so they remain secondary to striations.",
      "evidence": "Glacial geology literature notes that fossil dropstones are occasional and not present in all glaciated terrains, limiting their diagnostic value.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, among the given options, rocks that have many scratches provide the best sign that glaciers once covered an area.",
      "evidence": "Steps 1‑2 establish striations as a direct, specific marker; steps 3‑4 show the other options lack that specificity.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [2, 4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
